%%%%** Section 5.1
\subsection{USB 3.x: SuperSpeed Generations}

USB 3.0 released 2008; naming later revised by USB-IF:

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|r|}\hline
Official Name & Marketing Name & Max Speed \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 1 & SuperSpeed & 5 Gbps \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 2 & SuperSpeed 10G & 10 Gbps \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 2$\times$2 & SuperSpeed 20G & 20 Gbps \\\hline
\end{tabular}

SuperSpeed uses additional dedicated signal pairs separate from legacy D+/D-- pairs.

\includegraphics[width=4in]{usb_figs/00079.eps}

Superspeed uses 2 additional of the above pairs (+4wires)

one pair for each direction.

Same cable carries both SuperSpeed and USB 2.0 simultaneously.

Fully backwards compatible with USB 2.0 and 1.1.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 5.2
\subsection{USB Type-C Connector}

Introduced 2014. Required for USB 3.1 and later standards.

\begin{itemize}
        \item Reversible: no ``wrong way'' to insert
        \item Carries USB data, power, DisplayPort, HDMI, and PCIe
        (via Thunderbolt Alternate Mode)
        \item Connector shape alone does not guarantee speed ---
        cable rating determines actual capability
        \item EU mandated USB-C for consumer electronics (2024)
        \item Apple, Google, and most Android devices now use USB-C exclusively
\end{itemize}

