%%%%** Section 5.3
\subsection{USB Power Delivery (USB PD)}

USB can now supply significant power over the same data cable.

\begin{tabular}{|l|l|r|}\hline
Standard & Voltage / Current & Max Power \\\hline
USB 1.x / 2.0 & 5V / 0.5A & 2.5W \\\hline
USB 3.0 & 5V / 0.9A & 4.5W \\\hline
USB PD 2.0 (2012) & up to 20V / 5A & 100W \\\hline
USB PD 3.1 (2021) & up to 48V / 5A & 240W \\\hline
\end{tabular}

Voltage negotiated dynamically between charger and device.

Enables laptop charging, powered displays, and high-power tools
all over a single USB-C cable.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}

\includegraphics[width=4in]{usb_figs/USBPowerCurvesAcroname.png}
\\Source: Acroname.com


<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}




%%%%** Section 5.4
\subsection{USB4}

Released 2019.  Based on Intel Thunderbolt 3 specification (open-sourced to USB-IF).

\begin{itemize}
        \item USB-C connector required (no Type-A variant)
        \item Up to 40 Gbps (USB4 Gen 3$\times$2, dual-lane)
        \item Tunnels USB 3.2, DisplayPort 2.0, and PCIe simultaneously
        \item Backwards compatible with USB 3.2 and Thunderbolt 3
        \item USB4 Version 2.0 (2022): up to {\bf 80 Gbps}
\end{itemize}

Thunderbolt 4 (Intel, 2020) and Thunderbolt 5 (2023, 120 Gbps burst)
are supersets of USB4 with stricter certification requirements.

<s>\end{slide}
<s>\begin{slide}
%%%%** Section 5.5
\subsection{USB Version Summary}

\begin{tabular}{|l|r|c|}\hline
Version & Max Speed & Year \\\hline
USB 1.1 & 12 Mbps & 1998 \\\hline
USB 2.0 Hi-Speed & 480 Mbps & 2000 \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 1 & 5 Gbps & 2008 \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 2 & 10 Gbps & 2013 \\\hline
USB 3.2 Gen 2$\times$2 & 20 Gbps & 2017 \\\hline
USB4 Gen 3$\times$2 & 40 Gbps & 2019 \\\hline
USB4 v2.0 & 80 Gbps & 2022 \\\hline
\end{tabular}

Speed has increased $\sim$6,000$\times$ from USB 1.1 to USB4 v2.0 in 25 years.

USB-C connector + USB PD together enable a single cable for
power and data across virtually all modern consumer devices.

